WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.780
Everybody is talking about how terribly important
innovation

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:25.650
is. People are defining innovation. i think
the best

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.080
taxonomy of innovation I have seen is a paper
that's about

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.880
five years old now, if you are curious about
breaking

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.190
innovation down into its components, there
is a paper called

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.260
Darwin and the Demon by Geoffrey Moore which
essentially a

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.470
taxonomy of innovation, and it tells you - it
describes and

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.090
breaks innovation down into topics like service
innovation,

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.441
business process innovation, product innovation
and so on.

00:00:51.441 --> 00:00:55.690
And the fundamental message you might imagine
is the type of

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.570
innovation you work on is less important than
the fact that

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.470
you are really, really good at that type of
innovation and

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.880
if it's consistent with your company's key
business and

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.220
strategic initiatives, right. People tend
to think of Apple

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:09.951
as sort of the pinnacle of innovation. Arguably,
they are

00:01:09.951 --> 00:01:13.190
just one type of innovation, and that is product
innovation.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.630
So everybody is talking about what innovation
is. It's

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.700
always good to start with definitions. For
the purpose of

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.580
this discussion, innovation is creation resulting
from study

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.360
and experimentation, and I want to make a
distinction here.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.960
If you come over the cute idea, you put it
on the Wiki, you

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.709
throw it in a virtual suggestion box somewhere,
it might be

00:01:31.709 --> 00:01:33.890
very creative, it might be cute, it might
clever, but it's

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.050
not innovation. For legitimate innovation,
you need some

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.300
sort of instantiation of the idea, whether
it's a sketch or

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.000
storyboard related to some new service you
are thinking of,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.000
or a simple prototype of what you have created,
or a

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.060
physical layout, or some new space that you
are thinking

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.880
about. You need to have some sort of instantiation
for your

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:01.190
creative idea in order to actually foster
innovation. What I

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:05.090
want to talk about over the next hour and
I want to hear

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.810
from you about when I introduce some of the
ideas is some

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.290
specific methods to foster a culture of innovation
in your

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.780
organization. That is, I want to talk about
the 'how' rather

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.680
than the 'what' or the 'why'. First, let me
just give you

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.650
one background slide. I am Director of Technology
Strategy

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.350
at IDO. We are a - just so that you understand
where I am

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.240
coming from and what my perspective is, we
are professional

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.800
services firm, about 20 years old now. I joined
actually

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.240
when the company had just been created as
a result of a

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:40.510
merger with three other firms. And our exclusive
mission

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.080
statement is that we help our clients to innovate
as a

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.890
professional services firm, and we do that
by taking a

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:50.220
human-centered approach to design of products
and services

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.150
and experiences. People pay us to do three
things. People

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.760
pay us to gain insights into populations of
stakeholders.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.510
They might be internal people of an organization;
they might

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.510
be external customers. For example, one of
my projects now

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.330
has to do with better understanding the needs
of home PC

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:14.930
users in China. Second, people pay us to visualize
solutions, opportunities and solutions based

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.261
on the
inspiration that comes from that field of

00:03:18.261 --> 00:03:23.120
research. And
third, people pay us to help them redesign

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.220
their
organization using methods of design thinking

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:33.510
to help them
foster more sustainable cultures of innovation.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:40.120
So, in
preparing this, what David and I agreed would

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.300
be most
interesting are to highlight some of the methods

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.470
of what we
like to call Design Thinking and the power

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:51.460
of designing
thinking to drive innovation in an organization.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.410
What's the
big idea with design thinking? There are any

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.300
number of ways
to innovate; there are any number of ways

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.990
to be creative.
One way to do that is to look at some of the

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.610
most creative
professions in the world that are applied.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.360
Look at
advertising, look at architecture, look at

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.230
industrial design
and ask yourself "What are some of the mental

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.030
constructs
that these people have? How do they think?

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:15.290
How do they
experience the world?" And then, to roll those

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:18.060
approaches,
those behaviors that some of the most creative

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.280
people have
into a set of principles that we call Design

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.830
Thinking. And
that's what I want to talk about to you this

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:30.680
afternoon.
What's design thinking all about? Design thinking

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.979
is about
creating the right kind of interdisciplinary

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:35.530
teams of what
we call t-shaped people - we will get to that

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.050
in a minute.
That's one of the key points that I want to

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.290
make this
morning. The second key thing that I want

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:42.590
to emphasize is
the second area around design thinking, which

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:51.180
is to observe
to become inspired and to synthesize observations,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:55.249
sticking
up with actionable observations, actionable

00:04:55.249 --> 00:04:59.330
opportunities.
The third principle of design thinking is

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.610
to use your
inspiration gained from the field to visualize

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:06.050
new
opportunities. And the fourth is to make appropriate

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.810
use of
prototypes, and I am going to define prototypes

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:14.090
for you, to
help drive forward the innovation process.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:17.499
So we will go
into each of these in detail but I want to

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:19.520
make a very key
point here, and I think it was mentioned in

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.300
the blurb that
summarize this talk. What's the subtext here?

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.800
Okay, I am
talking about how to be creative, how to be

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.060
innovative up
here. The subtext here is innovation is absolutely

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.110
not some
innate talent that people either have or they

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.819
don't1. That's
this old archaic way of thinking about innovation

00:05:35.819 --> 00:05:37.780
and
creativity, and I urge you to reject it. I

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:44.150
completely reject
that notion. Innovation is absolutely a learnable

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:48.710
skill. As
with any other skill, like playing piano or

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.210
learning
mathematics, different people appear to have

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.650
different
levels of innate aptitudes towards creativity.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.770
I know a guy
who is a VC and has like a new idea, and it's

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.340
a pretty good
idea like every eight minutes. I call it the

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.150
Carter timer
because just like Carter has these new ideas

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:06.340
constantly.
That's great. The key point I want to make

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:11.539
is anyone can
learn the team-based sport of innovation.

00:06:11.539 --> 00:06:16.509
How many of you
worked with advertising firms? This is old

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.720
archaic notion in
many advertising firms, especially if you

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:20.879
like to watch Mad
Men, something like that, still

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:24.090
[inaudible]. The term 'creative' is a noun.
Have you guys

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.189
swerved into this? They use the word 'creative'
to describe

00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:30.180
these people in backrooms on Madison Avenue.
You never

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.279
really meet. They have got weird shoes and
messed up hair

00:06:32.279 --> 00:06:34.930
and stuff like that. And you meet the well-groomed
account

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.379
manager in an accounting firm. I see some
people nodding,

00:06:37.379 --> 00:06:39.509
you have been through this. And the premise
there is the

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:41.860
creatives are people that you can't possibly
be, and they

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.830
are locked away because they have something
you don't.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.620
Reject that. It's bunk. I have been in this
field for 20

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.699
years. I can tell you these are learnable
skills. How do you

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:54.819
learn them? What do you do? Step one, assemble
the right

00:06:54.819 --> 00:06:57.379
team. Assemble an innovation team that is
both

00:06:57.379 --> 00:07:01.900
interdisciplinary, and foster the creation
t-shaped people.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.110
Let's take those things in reverse order.
What's a t-shaped

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:10.409
person? A a-shaped person - and we use the
metaphor of a 't'

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:16.250
literally - a t-shaped person is someone who
has a depth of

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:20.719
expertise. No nothing middle managers are
gone, they

00:07:20.719 --> 00:07:25.100
disappeared in the 80s. You need to know something.
You are

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.050
deep in accounting; you are deep in finance;
you are all

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:28.990
about engineering; you are electrical engineer;
you are

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:30.650
software engineer; you are a chip designer.
You know

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.050
something. You have got a depth of expertise
that is your

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.809
path to move upwards into the professional
world. What's the

00:07:36.809 --> 00:07:41.809
legality? The legality is not being all things
to all

00:07:41.809 --> 00:07:48.699
people. The legality is honest appreciation
and empathy for

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:53.779
the other disciplines. So I am talking here
about a software

00:07:53.779 --> 00:07:57.020
engineer who is really, really good at writing
code but has

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.749
an honest appreciation for example of the
needs and the

00:08:01.749 --> 00:08:05.349
motivations of a marketing person and a salesperson
and a

00:08:05.349 --> 00:08:11.410
quality engineer. When you are hiring, and
when you are

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:15.150
growing talent as leaders in Silicon Valley
or elsewhere,

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.599
when you are interviewing, look for people
that use the

00:08:18.599 --> 00:08:22.199
pronoun 'we' more than they use the pronoun
'I'. Look for

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:25.990
people who are proud of their achievements
primarily by

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:29.089
highlighting their own contributions as part
of a team of

00:08:29.089 --> 00:08:32.210
people that they are really proud of. That's
an early

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:36.910
indicator that you are looking at a t-shaped
individual. You

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:40.149
will not get much in a way of innovation from
people who

00:08:40.149 --> 00:08:43.070
talk about how smart they are, and they might
be super smart

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:47.900
and sort of suddenly devalue the contributions
of other

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:49.960
people that were around them on the team or
their boss or

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:56.930
something like that. So, this sort of t-shaped
behavior is

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:01.280
really essential for successful innovation
teams. I spend a

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.820
lot of time working with David. David fundamentally
had a

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:05.990
program that he and I worked together last
summer. He was

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:08.150
fundamentally the business guy in a program.
We will talk

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.330
more about that in a minute. But the fact
that he had an

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:14.690
honest appreciation for the needs of the manufacturing
engineers who were worried about the cost

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:18.901
of the product
that he put out. So it's depth of expertise

00:09:18.901 --> 00:09:23.250
in an area, and
what I would call an honest and sincere empathy

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:28.750
for other
domains that are outside your own, that could

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.770
be cultivated
in people, that can also be looked for in

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:37.260
the hiring
process. So, what do we recommend for an innovation

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.900
team?
You want to do something innovative. You are

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.470
thinking about
next generation data services for smartphones.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:47.670
You are
thinking about some really new exciting renewable

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:51.700
energy
programs. You are thinking about fostering

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:53.830
products to
decrease the cost of health care. There are

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:57.180
just a
tremendous number of wonderful domains that

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.290
I am sure you
are either in or will be moving into when

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:03.620
you graduate that
are exciting and it will all call for tremendous

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.980
innovation,
little hard to read. Let me talk you through

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:12.990
the text here.
I submit that in early stages for innovation,

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.330
you need three
voices on an innovation team, and those voices

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:21.100
come from t-
shaped people who are open ultimately to unifying

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.090
into a
single voice and a single direction. There

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.070
are people who
have strong points of view themselves but

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.610
appreciate the
existence of other people's point of view.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:33.260
So what have we
got? We have got the voice of business and

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:38.630
business
viability, the voice of technology and the

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:42.630
voice of the
human, the voice of the stakeholder, either

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.780
the end customer
or the person somewhere else in the supply

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.250
chain who is an
important stakeholder in the business or policy

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:54.550
success of
your endeavor. Let's talk about each of those.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:57.640
Let's talk
about business briefly, and then one of the

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:00.970
things that
David recommended that we talk in some detail

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:04.640
about how as
business leaders, you can manage technical

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.470
people to help
foster innovation in highly technical organizations.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:14.300
But
let's start with business. What is the role

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.630
of a
businessperson on an innovation team? Before

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.900
we are getting
into the details of implementation, before

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.350
we are into sort
of classic product management areas of rollout

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:28.730
and so on, a
businessperson - and a lot of you may play

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:31.620
this role, those
of you that are still in school may play this

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:34.860
role in the
future - a businessperson does two things

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.540
on an innovation
team. First of all, a businessperson helps

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:42.770
acquaint every
single person on an innovation team - and

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:45.650
for small
innovation team, we are often talking 3 to

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:50.430
10 people - helps
acquaint and familiarize everyone adequately

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:54.010
with the
business landscape around the innovation challenge

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:58.660
that you
are facing. And this leads to the second myth

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.750
buster
statement I want to make which is that there

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.790
is this myth
that innovation thrives in, what is called,

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:09.060
the Greenfield
absent any boundaries. That's just wrong,

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.110
and that's another
notion that you should reject if you have

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:14.110
ever heard it.
Innovation thrives on constraints, and this

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:17.180
goes to a topic
that you mentioned a little earlier. Some

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.860
of the most
constrained cultures, and indeed constrained

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.790
businesses,
tiny broke startups with hardly any money,

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:25.720
there are
cultures. India is one of the - we are opening

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:30.480
up office in
Mumbai. We are seeing some of the most impressive

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.620
creativity
and innovation coming out of India that I

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.430
have ever seen in
my entire life. And a lot of that has to do

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:39.600
with scarcities
in India driving really exciting innovation.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.240
It has to do
with energy scarcity, and resource scarcity,

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:46.540
and capital
market scarcity, and so on. So keep in mind

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:50.840
that in general,
boundaries and constraints and limits are

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:55.360
the friend of
innovation. They help drive innovation. Any

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.000
program, from
the outset, should be operating under a clear

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.810
set of
boundaries and constraints from a businessperson

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.360
on the
current business landscape. Everybody in an

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.690
innovation team
should understand things like current market

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:12.000
segmentation,
and the competitive landscape, and the pricing

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.200
and costing
models, and the ultimate marketing plan. So

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.660
David, on our
program, part of his role was to act as an

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:25.060
ambassador to the
IDO team helping everybody develop the sort

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:28.840
of shared
conversions around business issues. The other

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:32.210
role that a
businessperson plays on many innovation teams,

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:35.520
depending on
the nature of what you are doing, is acting

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:41.010
as a strategic
advisor to the external partners that you

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:44.670
are working with.
So again, using the project that was related

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:47.840
to crafting,
the home crafting market, that David and I

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:51.580
worked on over
the summer. David also acted as a strategic

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:56.890
advisor to our
client building some quantitative spreadsheets

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:00.430
to help
better understand the potential of the proposals

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.550
we were
coming up with as compared to their existing

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:06.420
product line.
So, that's what - I mean in brief, we could

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:10.230
talk about this
for hours, all afternoon - but in brief, that's

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:13.330
what a
businessperson does. The inward facing role

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:17.710
of a
businessperson is to help familiarize everybody

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:20.080
on an
interdisciplinary team with business issues.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:22.920
The outward
facing role is to act as a strategic advisor

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:27.200
to partners as
appropriate. Now, let's talk in little more

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:32.060
detail about
technologists on innovation teams. This is

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:34.530
something that
David and I agreed would be an interesting

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:38.931
thing to cover.
It's very likely that all of you will be in

00:14:38.931 --> 00:14:44.200
a position of
managing technologists and wanting to gain

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:48.090
maximum value
from technologists, engineers, scientists,

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:53.590
technicians on
innovation programs. I want to make a couple

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:58.860
of points here.
The first is, I would like to draw a distinction

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.830
between
technology push and technology pull. And just

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:08.260
to cut to the
chase, I submit that the winners - and this

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:10.550
has been
consistent, this is one of the few consistent

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.900
things over
the 20 years I have had at IDO, so much has

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:17.600
changed -
consistently, the winners are the ones who

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.910
create a healthy
tension between technology push and technology

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:24.900
pull and
consider both simultaneously. What does that

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:28.520
mean in a
little more detail? Technology push is a philosophy

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:32.700
which
says, let's take our existing assets, let's

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:35.290
take our
intellectual property, let's take technology

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:38.960
that we have
developed, and let's understand new domains

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:43.710
of opportunity
for that technology, let's identify new areas

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.450
around the
globe, new geographic regions that might be

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.380
interested, let'
s identify new vertical markets where we might

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:54.660
apply that
technology, let's identify new scenarios of

00:15:54.660 --> 00:16:01.670
use. This is an
incredibly important and viable business model

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:03.450
in and of
itself in isolation.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.580
[Informal Talk]

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:12.260
So Professor Andrew
[inaudible] sees tech push as core to the

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:17.800
majority if not
all of the text startups coming out of Silicon

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:21.400
Valley. My
personal poster child for technology push

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.540
is Qualcomm. Some
of you may have worked with them. They are

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.800
a telecom company
based out of San Diego. They have fantastic

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:31.220
technologies.
One is called CDMA, it's a wireless protocol.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:32.890
Qualcomm said
to themselves, we have got this fantastic

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:36.700
wireless protocol,
let's push it into new domains. We are starting

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.650
to saturate
the United States, let's push into Korea,

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:41.990
let's push into
China. We are starting to saturate the telecom

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.610
market in
general, let's push into new vertical markets,

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.500
let's push
into medical instruments and devices, body

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.980
worn devices like
cardiac monitors that broadcast their telemetry

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:53.590
back to
mission control centers. It's been a hugely

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:57.100
successful model
for them. Now, let's contrast tech push with

00:16:57.100 --> 00:17:00.970
technology
pull. Technology pull says, let's forget about

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:03.980
technology,
let's forget about what we own. Let's put

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:09.539
business models
and human beings at the center of our thinking.

00:17:09.539 --> 00:17:13.860
And let's
think about important and relevant trends,

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:18.230
demographic,
economic, social, political, religious. Let's

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:21.490
think about
important emerging trends, and let's ask ourselves

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:23.870
what
technology might be brought to bear on those

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:27.470
trends that is
relevant. Or more typically, what aggregation

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:31.740
of technology
might be brought to bear on those trends that

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:35.420
is relevant to
this fundamental consumer demand, this fundamental

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:40.480
human
need. Poster child here, longtime client of

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:45.250
IDO's, Procter &amp;
Gamble. So they employ thousands of scientists

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:49.110
but they don'
t typically start with a given science with

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.770
a given
technology that they have developed, like

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:53.559
a chemical
formulation, and explore how to apply it.

00:17:53.559 --> 00:17:57.460
They typically
start with fundamental human needs like people

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:04.120
hate ironing
or people want their clothes to last longer,

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:06.780
and they ask
themselves what technology can be brought

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:13.480
to bear. So, the
interesting thing is if you go back and look

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:17.620
at my two
poster children for these two trends, what's

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:23.200
Qualcomm doing?
Qualcomm is making a very hard move into technology

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:26.490
pull.
Qualcomm is hiring market researchers and

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:30.220
anthropologists
and human factors people like crazy in order

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:32.730
to better
understand some of the global social and economic

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:35.160
trends,
and they are coming up with a philosophy that

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:39.240
includes
organic development of technology, licensing

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:43.730
and outsourced
technology development with a view towards

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:48.690
increasing their
quotient around technology pull. And similarly,

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:51.190
Procter &amp;
Gamble, right about the time I started working

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:54.580
with them
which is about 12 years ago, they are moving

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:58.690
this way. They
had developed a certain technology for de-wrinkling

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:00.891
governments, and they said to them - and what
they

00:19:00.891 --> 00:19:03.550
essentially said to IDO was where else can
this technology

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:07.530
be applied, how can we push this technology
to new domains

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:09.490
and maximize the investment that we have already
put into

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:15.240
this technology. So, let me just show you
a couple of

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.790
examples of technology pull that I think are
significant.

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:22.210
All of you have probably seen technology push.
What's hot

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:25.400
these days? There is a technology that you
have probably

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:28.710
heard about. If not, you will call Near Field
Communication,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:34.820
NFC. This is an important enabling technology
for

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:38.960
transactions and mobile commerce, typically
from a cell

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:44.331
phone, for the mobile phone. So, that sort
of technology

00:19:44.331 --> 00:19:48.090
lends itself to say, okay, there is an existing
Near Field

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.510
Communication technology, one of them for
example is called

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:52.090
Sony FeliCa. What else can we do with it?
What else can be

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:57.020
pushed? Good viable way to look at the world.
The first

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:01.150
example I want to bring up for tech pull is
entirely new

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:06.350
mental models of authority and expertise.
I am older than a

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:09.480
lot of you guys, and I can tell you that the
mental

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:13.440
constructs that the world has around authority
and expertise

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:16.080
have been turned on their head in the last
15 years,

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:19.020
something that sociologists and historians
are going to be

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:24.630
studying forever. Traditional models of authority,
doctors,

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:29.810
ministers, consumer reports, magazines, on
losing power in

00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:34.560
favor of bottoms up/ground up authority from
Wikis and

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:37.200
blogs, and crowdsourcing. I mean my God, look
what's

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:41.300
happened in Egypt, if anybody more than two
weeks ago had

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:43.710
any doubt about the importance of the inversion
of

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:49.520
authority. Look at some of these examples,
public

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:52.520
commissioning investigative reports on Spot.Us,
live

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:56.470
broadcast from mobile web phones, a TV station
based

00:20:56.470 --> 00:21:00.910
entirely on user-created content. So this
is not a

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:03.670
technology like a Wiki or a blog or something
like this.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:08.620
This is a fundamental human trend, and it
is incumbent on us

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:11.530
to ask ourselves all right, what technology
could be brought

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:14.410
to bear by my organization or by the startup
I am creating

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:19.270
or whatever to address this important trend.
Another

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:25.290
example, all the worlds are pointer. This
is not a

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:28.100
technology, it's a trend, it's a new mental
construct. It's

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:30.440
a technology-driven construct because that's
what I

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:35.670
specialize in, and we could talk about non-technology-driven
constructs. People are expecting - they wouldn't

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:38.210
put it to
you this way - but people are expecting a

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:42.550
virtual overlay to
the physical world, and it's been fascinating

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:48.760
for me to
watch this grow. 10 years ago, this thing

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:51.650
called Yellow
Arrow was really huge, and we all thought

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:54.120
it was just
amazing and very, very cool. I mean 10 years

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:59.900
just seems to
fly by. But the state of the art of augmented

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:02.550
reality 10
years ago was people would slap these yellow

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.460
arrow stickers
like in front of historic buildings in New

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:10.010
York. You would
text message the serial number, the unique

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:13.150
serial number on
that yellow arrow into the yellow arrow company,

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.130
and you
would get a text message back which allowed

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:18.559
you to read in
just straight text about what you were looking

00:22:18.559 --> 00:22:21.110
at. Now, 10
years later, we have got things like Google

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:24.530
Goggles that
allows you to actually hold your phone up

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:29.210
in front of
something and see a virtual overlay to the

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:31.850
physical world,
like the ratings of a given restaurant or

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:34.900
something like
that. Much of this is enabled by Cloud computing.

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:37.620
So here
again, why we bring this up? We bring this

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:42.320
up because this
is an important technology pull mental construct.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:45.840
People are
going to start to think of physical objects

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:50.050
as tags and
pointers to cyber space. That's a very important

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:52.890
trend and
it's only going to increase. Everybody is

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.340
thinking about
this. I am working with State Farm Insurance

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:59.730
right now, we
spend a lot of our time on my current project

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:03.110
thinking about
the previous trend I described to you which

00:23:03.110 --> 00:23:07.660
is the notion of
what happens to the insurance business in

00:23:07.660 --> 00:23:11.870
an era in which
authority is inverted and access to information,

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:15.620
like
actuarial tables, has become completely democratized.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:18.620
I want
to pause there because that's the first major

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:20.920
thing I wanted
to talk about was this. Well, let me just

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:23.429
say a few more
words about tech push and tech pull just to

00:23:23.429 --> 00:23:26.370
sum up. So what
are we talking about here? We are talking

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:30.080
about managing
technologists, and we are talking about managing

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:33.490
engineers
and how engineers need to behave on innovation

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:36.870
programs. And
so again, the first point I want to make here

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:41.100
is that one
job of an engineer or a technologist managed

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:44.390
by a
businessperson, which most of you are, is

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:49.950
to help evaluate
important trends through a lens of relevant

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:53.610
technologies in
order to spell out business implications,

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:58.390
large scale social
needs, important trends and so on. Thoughts

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:02.530
to this point, I
want to pause here, thoughts on tech push

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:03.530
versus tech pull.

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:04.530
[Audience]

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:05.530
You can use tech pull to drive major R&amp;D decisions
in an

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:06.530
organization and tech pull lends itself better
to that than

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:23.640
tech push. Tech push deals mostly with what's
out today and

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:42.950
what the emerging - what the trajectories
are of, say, these

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:54.130
[inaudible] materials and so on. Whereas,
tech pull leads

00:24:54.130 --> 00:25:01.400
you to think all right, what are some important
emerging

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:03.370
trends and what type of development ad what
sort of future

00:25:03.370 --> 00:25:08.970
technology is required for my organization
to avail itself

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:16.130
to those trends.

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:18.510
[Audience]

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:31.940
So technology is so vast that people are - any
technologist

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:33.640
is limited by his or her toolbox. I mean there
are a couple

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:43.120
of things you can do. First of all, you can
hire people who

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:46.200
are intellectual, curious and open to constantly
informing

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:48.929
themselves on all kinds of emerging trends.
So even though

00:25:48.929 --> 00:25:53.340
that is by definition I going to be a toolbox,
people who

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:59.040
are extremely focused and committed to a certain
type of

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:02.530
technology or certain type of implementation
are incredibly

00:26:02.530 --> 00:26:04.530
important, valuable to any organization. You
need people

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:10.190
like that but they are not good fits for innovation
programs. So the first thing I would say is

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:13.950
just staff
carefully. Staff someone who loves reading

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:17.610
The Economist
magazine, who is always interested in broadly

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:21.030
informing
himself or herself. Also keep your Rolodex

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:25.190
really fat full
of experts. I mean my dirty little secret

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:28.960
is a lot of what I
am able to do is digging to my Rolodex because

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:31.299
I've been at
this for so long. So when someone needs an

00:26:31.299 --> 00:26:34.950
expert on RFID
chips, I have got a guy. And when someone

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:38.000
needs someone who
is an expert on artificial intelligence, I

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:40.610
have got someone
else. In every case, what characterizes those

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:48.910
people is they
know how to bring interdisciplinary innovation

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:52.330
teams up to a
shared level of familiarity without bearing

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:56.010
them in
technical detail that they don't understand

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:57.010
anyway. Other
questions?

00:26:57.010 --> 00:27:01.940
[Audience]

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:09.670
Yeah, that's a good point. You want to have
wide Rolodex,

00:27:09.670 --> 00:27:10.670
yeah. And of course, that's easier and easier
with

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:27.750
information technology these days. But you
still have to

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:35.480
have a right kind of person, a good cultural
fit that can

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:36.480
assist
[inaudible].

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:37.480
[Audience]

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:38.480
Yeah, we are going to cover that in the next
session. Yeah,

00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:39.480
that's a very important point because the
businesspeople and

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:45.270
the technical people need to change their
behavior depending

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:48.520
on the themes of the program. Your behavior
is static any

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:50.940
more, then the development phases of the program
are static.

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:54.660
Okay, no other questions, we move on with
that. So I guess

00:27:54.660 --> 00:28:00.290
one - here is how I like to think about what
I call the

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:03.679
dynamic role a technologist, and you could
just as easily

00:28:03.679 --> 00:28:10.510
call this the dynamic role of a businessperson.
Because a

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.370
lot of you are interested in leadership, some
of you are

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:15.670
already in leadership, in many cases, you
are going to be

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:22.900
managing heavily technical teams. Something
that has been

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:26.510
difficult for me and that has been a personal
transformation

00:28:26.510 --> 00:28:29.260
for me, and that is incumbent on many of the
technologists

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:33.950
is what is to adjust your behavior, your patterns
of

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:37.419
behavior, what you say and what you do, depending
on the

00:28:37.419 --> 00:28:40.429
phase of the program. How many of your are
engineers in the

00:28:40.429 --> 00:28:42.801
room? How many of you have an engineering
background? Who

00:28:42.801 --> 00:28:47.111
saw the Tacoma Narrows video? Can you describe
it to

00:28:47.111 --> 00:28:48.111
everybody?

00:28:48.111 --> 00:29:19.120
[Audience]

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:30.679
Yeah, 

00:29:30.679 --> 00:29:40.470
I mean the bridge is completed, and it goes
crashing

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:44.049
into this canyon below, it tears - yeah, and
the rebar gets

00:29:44.049 --> 00:29:46.429
torn open and things like that. So basically,
all engineers

00:29:46.429 --> 00:29:50.290
have seen this video, and the message behind
this video is

00:29:50.290 --> 00:29:54.130
don't let this happen to you. Be conservative;
be risk

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:58.679
diverse; be safe, use safety margins. And
that's exactly as

00:29:58.679 --> 00:30:04.450
it should be. When you are in the development
program, the

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:07.559
development stage of a program. If I am driving
over a

00:30:07.559 --> 00:30:10.050
bridge with my family, I want it to be just
having been

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:15.530
designed by a professional engineer with large
safety

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:19.410
margins. When I carry my laptop computer on
to a plane, I

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:20.950
want it to have been designed by someone who
knows how to

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:28.200
treat lithium batteries so that they don't
explode. But

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:31.180
fundamentally, engineering - and I am an electrical
engineer

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:35.179
by training, both undergrad and grad - engineering
is

00:30:35.179 --> 00:30:38.910
fundamentally a risk-averse profession, and
that's the issue

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:42.280
that needs to change for a successful and
consistent

00:30:42.280 --> 00:30:46.010
innovation. Here is a deal. This is a very
qualitative graph

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:50.870
that I have drawn. These are three major phases
of any

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:53.980
program with the innovation elements highlighted.
What do we

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:58.650
do? We explore, we ideate, and then we develop.
Now, if

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:02.750
these were linear, this is 95% of the project,
this little

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:05.910
block would go like all the way through that
wall to the

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:12.440
outside. But these phases, these early innovation
phases are

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:16.010
desperately important and that's why I wanted
to call them

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:20.170
out. What's important is shown vertically
and quantitatively

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:23.490
here. So what's important when you are doing
development?

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:25.281
What matters is that you don't have another
Tacoma Narrows.

00:31:25.281 --> 00:31:27.539
What matters is that your lithium battery
doesn't explode or

00:31:27.539 --> 00:31:31.490
that your bridge doesn't fall into the canyon.
So, an

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:33.980
attitude of feasibility and risk mitigation,
which all

00:31:33.980 --> 00:31:37.750
engineers are trained in and the field of
tracks people who

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:40.950
are careful and want to mitigate risk, is
of prime

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:47.090
importance during the development phase. If
I come up to

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:50.410
Lewis, and he has got a circuit board that's
almost done,

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:53.020
and it's got a shape, and I say to him, you
know what, I

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:54.580
have just discovered a new chip that I think
would do a

00:31:54.580 --> 00:31:56.690
better job for you, and I think you should
switch over to

00:31:56.690 --> 00:32:02.309
it. I may be correct factually. It may be
a better chip, it

00:32:02.309 --> 00:32:05.710
may be a higher processing power and user
fewer watts. But

00:32:05.710 --> 00:32:08.090
it's a stupid thing to say because we are
in development,

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:10.480
and we are going into regulatory testing,
and we have got to

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:12.880
get the thing out in the market in four weeks
or six weeks.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:17.740
So I am saying a correct thing but at the
wrong time. Now,

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:20.299
let's talk about the inwards of that. This
is where things

00:32:20.299 --> 00:32:23.880
really go wrong involving technical people
in innovation

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:27.530
generally, particularly in big companies,
a question came up

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:32.309
around big companies. During the exploration
phase, as if

00:32:32.309 --> 00:32:34.919
you are interested in leading innovation,
you have got to

00:32:34.919 --> 00:32:39.830
figure out a way for people to extend disbelief
for days or

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:43.120
weeks during the exploration phase, and in
particular you

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:45.660
have got a figure out a way for people to
think about new

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:49.400
technologies, new and emerging technologies
in terms of

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:53.880
inspiration and opportunities, not in terms
of the risks

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.360
that they represent. For those of you that
are not

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:01.010
engineers, engineering training, which is
important, is a

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:04.230
great deal if it is around risk mitigation.
If it asks to

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:07.850
deliver something, you pluck out the areas
of risk. If you

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:11.250
are asked to deliver a medical instrument,
you say okay, the

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:14.800
fluid handling system is going to be problematic
and these

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:16.940
sensitive analog electronics are going to
be an issue. I am

00:33:16.940 --> 00:33:19.490
going to pluck out those risk areas, and I
am going to worry

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:21.530
about them, and I am going to mitigate the
risks with

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:26.260
analysis and iterative testing and iterative
prototyping.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:29.790
That's great. That's as it should be. During
the early phase

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:33.289
of a program, you need to figure out a way
for technical

00:33:33.289 --> 00:33:36.809
people and technical teams to think about
emerging

00:33:36.809 --> 00:33:39.110
technologies in terms of the opportunity and
in terms of the

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:41.870
potential for inspiration of those emerging
technologies,

00:33:41.870 --> 00:33:46.970
and not in terms of the risks they represent.
I am talking

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:49.750
about the cultural change here. I am talking
about a

00:33:49.750 --> 00:33:54.010
behavioral change that is born of people.
I mean what is

00:33:54.010 --> 00:33:58.580
culture? A culture is a result of what people
do and what

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:01.690
they say in a given organization. I am talking
about people

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:05.590
saying different things and doing different
things as

00:34:05.590 --> 00:34:08.849
appropriate during different phases of the
program. So the

00:34:08.849 --> 00:34:11.629
counter example of what I mentioned - the
sort of

00:34:11.629 --> 00:34:15.750
hypothetical I gave with Lewis is this. If
during the

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:20.839
exploration phase, Lewis brings up a really
exciting idea

00:34:20.839 --> 00:34:24.709
for a mobile data service, and I say you know
what, there is

00:34:24.709 --> 00:34:27.011
a reason that it's never going to work or
the reason it

00:34:27.011 --> 00:34:29.769
would just be too expensive to develop, Lewis,
or something

00:34:29.769 --> 00:34:34.919
like that. I may be correct, my statement
may be rigorous

00:34:34.919 --> 00:34:38.730
and factually correct, but I am saying it
at the wrong time

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:43.330
because what I need to do is create an environment
[inaudible] disbelief is suspended for an

00:34:43.330 --> 00:34:49.580
extended amount of
time. If you allow statements like well, we

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:51.980
tried that
before and it doesn't really work, classic

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:55.270
big company
stuff, or that's really risky or hey, you

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:57.700
know, that will
never get through management, those statements

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:00.701
may be but
you can't allow that during the early phases

00:35:00.701 --> 00:35:03.050
of a program,
otherwise you will definitely, definitely

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:06.180
squelch
innovation, you will never get any fresh ideas,

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:10.220
and you will
never bring anything disruptive to the market.

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:13.830
And then,
again, qualitatively, in the middle during

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:18.840
ideation, what
you want to manage is essentially equal importance

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:22.000
to both
of these styles of thinking. So for example,

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:24.620
you want to
think about emerging technology or about business

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:26.810
risk in
the case of business to take David's question

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:30.670
from a little
earlier. You want to think about those issues

00:35:30.670 --> 00:35:34.490
on one hand in
terms of opportunities and inspiration for

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:37.580
new products and
new services and so on. And on the other hand,

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:41.440
you want to
honestly assess the risks inherent in, say,

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:47.840
the new business
model or latching on to some emerging technology.

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:52.550
So, what
do technologists on innovation team do and

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:55.410
how do they
recommend you manage technologists during

00:35:55.410 --> 00:36:00.130
the innovation
phase? Technologists should be helping everybody

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:02.609
on an
interdisciplinary team to understand a broad

00:36:02.609 --> 00:36:07.240
aggregation of
emerging technologies. Technologists and businesspeople,

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:10.510
David is a great example, should act as coaches
and teachers

00:36:10.510 --> 00:36:14.690
to the team. If they are acting as policemen
during that

00:36:14.690 --> 00:36:21.800
early phase, you are in trouble. So when we
developed a

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:28.250
Voice over IP phone for Cisco, I think we
did an extremely

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:33.030
good job of doing two things. One was bringing
the

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:35.810
engineers, the scientists, all the technical
people as well

00:36:35.810 --> 00:36:38.560
as the marketing and human factors and businesspeople
out

00:36:38.560 --> 00:36:41.400
into the field to meet with a total of about
78 users in

00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:45.020
Europe, and Asia, and the U.S. And what I
can honestly say

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:48.221
is that some of the reasons that some emerging
technology

00:36:48.221 --> 00:36:52.580
was built into this new phone is because we
created a

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:56.950
careful environment in which disbelief was
suspended during

00:36:56.950 --> 00:37:00.490
early phases, and we looked very honestly
at technologies

00:37:00.490 --> 00:37:04.410
that, at that point, were still emerging,
like E Ink, the

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:10.090
technology used in a kindle, as kind of the
technologies for

00:37:10.090 --> 00:37:14.220
the phone. So, I would like to hear your thoughts
just about

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:18.170
this notion of your responsibility as leaders
to foster a

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:22.830
certain dynamic behavior of technologists,
a behavior that

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:25.060
shifts according to the stage of the program
that you are

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:30.830
in. It's easy to say, it is not an easy thing
to do. Yeah.

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:36.080
[Audience]

00:37:36.080 --> 00:38:04.250
Coach and a teacher, yeah, I mean if people
feel like look,

00:38:04.250 --> 00:38:08.690
I am going to write the best code in the world
and then hey,

00:38:08.690 --> 00:38:13.290
you are a business guy, your job is to put
a price on it and

00:38:13.290 --> 00:38:15.390
to manage the marketing activities. That's
okay. But if I am

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:20.350
saying that to you, I am really missing a
trick in terms of

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:22.670
innovation opportunities because if you are
around

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:29.000
familiarizing me with emerging Web 2.0 business
models and

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:32.660
familiarizing me with possible end markets
and new scenarios

00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:35.570
of use of whatever technology I am developing
that I never

00:38:35.570 --> 00:38:42.070
even thought of, it's going to benefit me.
If I feel like

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:44.790
you are going to create my style, you are
going to be a

00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:48.400
policeman, you are going to slow me down,
I am going to

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:50.390
avoid having business people in and I am going
to put

00:38:50.390 --> 00:38:53.670
technology on a pedestal and just sort of
blankly assume the

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:57.369
technology will sell itself. Part of the issue
there is

00:38:57.369 --> 00:39:00.510
being a T-shaped business person, having an
honesty empathy

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:04.050
and appreciation for the pressures and responsibilities
of

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:06.740
the technical people and part of it is positioning
yourself

00:39:06.740 --> 00:39:10.109
from the beginning as a supportive coach and
teacher,

00:39:10.109 --> 00:39:13.260
helping them to do their jobs better and get
it right the

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:14.550
first time. Other questions?

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:25.070
Audience

00:39:25.070 --> 00:40:07.690
To development resources, excellent development
resources

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:12.500
come on the late part of that curve. You typically
bring

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:16.620
them in when development is happening. But
other resources

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:20.270
can come in really early and my recommendation
is there is

00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:25.320
unique answer for every organization, but
my recommendation

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:28.920
here is every company should have a strategy
for innovation

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:32.950
and I mentioned it's
[inaudible]. There are only three ways to

00:40:32.950 --> 00:40:43.590
innovate. You can
in-source innovation, right. For those who

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:47.940
did watch GE,
like when it was six years ago, when Immelt

00:40:47.940 --> 00:40:54.190
took over, one
of the major incidents he had was to shift

00:40:54.190 --> 00:40:59.520
the innovation
process to try to pump up internal and organic

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:03.089
processes for
innovation. So how you are going to innovate?

00:41:03.089 --> 00:41:05.599
You can in-
source, you can outsource or you can hire

00:41:05.599 --> 00:41:09.240
companies like us
or McKenzie or someone like that to come up

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:12.830
with creative
solutions for you, and you can buy innovation,

00:41:12.830 --> 00:41:13.830
you can
aquire innovation, Cisco style, it's a very-very

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:19.180
successful
model. You can acquire, you can license, you

00:41:19.180 --> 00:41:23.839
can purchase.
So that's it for innovation and what I would

00:41:23.839 --> 00:41:26.770
say is we would
have to get into the specifics of your business

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:28.940
to
understand when it's appropriate to bring

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:32.480
in external
resources, but it should be guided by a set

00:41:32.480 --> 00:41:35.960
of strategic
principles around how you want to innovate,

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:40.680
right. And I
consider every single one of these fair game

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:50.080
and I can point
to companies that are extremely successful,

00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:53.250
being 90%, any
one of these. I can point to companies that

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:57.530
do most of their
innovations through in-sourcing and through

00:41:57.530 --> 00:42:00.700
internal growth,
that outsource most of their innovation to

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:04.609
design firms and
that innovate essentially by acquisitions

00:42:04.609 --> 00:42:07.500
and allow small
companies to take the innovation risk, they

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:09.110
are all good
workable models.

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:10.110
[Audience]

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:23.450
Yeah, you lead by example and you create a
culture and if

00:42:23.450 --> 00:42:48.260
they are qualitative or worse for the right
kind of

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:51.230
behavior. I mean I think you just need to
look at simple

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:55.690
economic style incentives, it's not literally
payment, they

00:42:55.690 --> 00:42:59.260
are intangible incentives. But I think you
just need to look

00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:04.530
at the fact that, I think you just need to
consider, I mean

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:07.770
if you are me, you consider that human beings
are entities

00:43:07.770 --> 00:43:11.080
that respond to incentives. And if the incentives
are there

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:13.730
for a particular type of behavior, you are
going to get it,

00:43:13.730 --> 00:43:19.150
they are going to tell you at
[inaudible] the incentives are there for well

00:43:19.150 --> 00:43:26.570
appropriate,
extended suspension of disbelief. And if you

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:30.290
come off as a
cynic and a naysayer, in some organizations

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:33.280
you come up
smart. A hardcore technical organization,

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:38.910
lots of PhDs built
around academic rigor, PhDs from a culture

00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:43.730
of peer review at
universities. If you can spear someone else's

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:48.010
idea and show
why it's wrong, you win, okay. That's important

00:43:48.010 --> 00:43:52.700
idea, that
comes into it, criticism comes into it, but

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:54.310
if you behave
the way at the wrong time, no one is going

00:43:54.310 --> 00:43:57.780
to want you in a
project at IDO, so you get your measure for,

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:00.220
you get your
reward to a great extent. And so you can start

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:02.650
by setting
the right example of yourself as members of

00:44:02.650 --> 00:44:08.349
leadership in
organizations. What else can I tell you about?

00:44:08.349 --> 00:44:15.560
Okay, I want to talk a little about Synthesis,
this is the

00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:18.410
third point that David want to make sure that
all he came

00:44:18.410 --> 00:44:20.920
away with because he thought it was an interesting
thing

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:25.810
that he had learned. So let's talk about inspiration
and

00:44:25.810 --> 00:44:29.250
let's talk about managing that inspiration
with a process

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:33.770
called Synthesis. How do you do it? First
of all, it's

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:36.460
important to define terms. I defined innovation
earlier. It'

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:42.339
s kind of weird how many CEOs and executives
talk about

00:44:42.339 --> 00:44:44.900
innovations and important plank in their strategy,
but can't

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:48.780
actually define what they mean by innovation.
Similarly, we

00:44:48.780 --> 00:44:51.109
have talked about inspiration a lot. We will
be talking

00:44:51.109 --> 00:44:57.950
about inspiration in this section. Inspiration
is people or

00:44:57.950 --> 00:45:04.320
things or events that influence the mind or
the creative

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:08.730
imagination. That's a very general definition.
That's how we

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:13.150
think of inspiration. They are people, they
are events, they

00:45:13.150 --> 00:45:16.589
are things that influence the mind or the
creative

00:45:16.589 --> 00:45:20.270
imagination. Now, one of the questions that
came up earlier

00:45:20.270 --> 00:45:22.650
was how can you be efficient, how can you
innovate in a

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:25.330
small organization, you got a limited budget,
you are in a

00:45:25.330 --> 00:45:27.560
hurry, maybe you are creating a startup in
you spare time

00:45:27.560 --> 00:45:32.830
and you have full time job. Here is what I
think you should

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:38.130
keep in mind. If you want to innovate, you
need to be

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:41.000
inspired. Like I mentioned earlier, I know
a guy that comes

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:44.510
up with a cool idea every eight minutes or
whatever, most

00:45:44.510 --> 00:45:47.750
people aren't like that, but all of us can
be creative and

00:45:47.750 --> 00:45:51.070
innovative if we are inspired. And if the
sources of

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:55.589
inspiration I mentioned earlier, by far the
strongest one

00:45:55.589 --> 00:45:58.300
are other human beings, face-to-face contact
with other

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:03.780
human beings. Some of you work in B2C businesses.
Of this

00:46:03.780 --> 00:46:08.080
role, a gut level of appreciation for the
needs of diverse

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:10.930
base of customers, not for meeting white papers,
not for

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:13.770
meeting market research, but from getting
out in the field

00:46:13.770 --> 00:46:18.150
and understanding how people actually live
and work and play

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:23.380
is terribly important. Some of you work in
B2B environments,

00:46:23.380 --> 00:46:26.920
for that, forget the word end-customer or
end-user, think

00:46:26.920 --> 00:46:30.400
about your stakeholders. You work for a software
company,

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:34.310
it's a B2B firm, you work for SAP, somebody
like that, fine.

00:46:34.310 --> 00:46:37.849
Let's get a visceral, let's get a heartfelt
appreciation for

00:46:37.849 --> 00:46:40.760
the needs of the business decision makers
and the technical

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:44.510
decision makers and some of the end users
that are going to

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:48.670
be use our software. In either case, what
you learn out in

00:46:48.670 --> 00:46:51.780
the field will surprise you, don't kid yourself
and you know

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:56.200
what really goes on. And the notion here is
very simple. If

00:46:56.200 --> 00:47:00.190
we are going to be inspired, let's use our
ability as human

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:03.430
beings to listen to what people have to say
and let's look

00:47:03.430 --> 00:47:06.359
at what they do, alright. Let's look at how
they use their

00:47:06.359 --> 00:47:09.550
cell phones, let's look at how they interact
with software,

00:47:09.550 --> 00:47:12.869
let's look at how they use emergency services
and let's use

00:47:12.869 --> 00:47:20.960
those observations of patterns of speech in
[inaudible] behavior to project and imagine

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:24.820
how people are
thinking and how people are feeling. So I

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:28.680
want to
distinguish when you are taking an information

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:30.780
early in an
innovation program, I want to distinguish

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:36.170
between classic
research which is essential and terribly important.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:39.770
This is
something that David did a wonderful job commissioning

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:43.350
from
two different market research sources at the

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:46.400
beginning of
the program we worked on, that's essential

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:49.280
background for
the team. But I don't think you would say

00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:52.770
that it inspired
us, it informed us and it was important for

00:47:52.770 --> 00:47:55.550
that reason, but
it didn't inspire us. What inspired us was

00:47:55.550 --> 00:47:58.360
getting out into
the field speaking to crafters and seeing

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:01.080
how they use
various types of crafting equipment. So I

00:48:01.080 --> 00:48:02.440
will give
everybody a minute to just read this off at

00:48:02.440 --> 00:48:06.920
the screen to
drive home the distinction between qualitative

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.890
research
intended to inspire and quantitative research

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:10.890
intended to
inform.

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:32.900
Okay. So, what's an example here? Shimano,
a lot of you are

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:36.440
public bikers, Shimano makes really nice hubs
and gear-sets,

00:48:36.440 --> 00:48:41.940
a lot of bike parts. Shimano asked us to think
about new

00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:48.160
opportunities in biking. And one of the things
we discovered

00:48:48.160 --> 00:48:51.210
and when you look back on these things obviously
in

00:48:51.210 --> 00:48:53.760
retrospect, you can
[inaudible] quarterback yourself and say,

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:56.380
oh, intuitively
that should have been obvious. We have no

00:48:56.380 --> 00:49:00.050
idea how many tens
of millions of Americans there were, who really

00:49:00.050 --> 00:49:03.550
want a bike,
they have happy memories of bicycle when they

00:49:03.550 --> 00:49:06.420
were kids, but
they are intimated by the state of bicycling

00:49:06.420 --> 00:49:08.950
today. They are
intimated when they walk into a bike shop,

00:49:08.950 --> 00:49:10.950
they are
intimated by the way the bikes look, the carbon

00:49:10.950 --> 00:49:13.380
fiber book
look, looks they are intimated by the ripped

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:17.530
guy in lycra
that's selling them the bikes and they don't

00:49:17.530 --> 00:49:20.589
want any part
of it. And so one of the things that came

00:49:20.589 --> 00:49:28.250
up was how can we
gain, how can we use needs-based insights

00:49:28.250 --> 00:49:31.451
from the field to
come up with a new type of bike. And what

00:49:31.451 --> 00:49:34.790
we came up with in
this case is what's called the Coaster Bike.

00:49:34.790 --> 00:49:38.020
And I can point
to a lot of simple little observations. This

00:49:38.020 --> 00:49:41.500
is an early
sketch of one of the bike seats. People would

00:49:41.500 --> 00:49:44.730
say look,
biking seems good, but it really hurts when

00:49:44.730 --> 00:49:48.119
I have my wallet
and my cell phone. So we created, I can show

00:49:48.119 --> 00:49:50.920
you an idea we
created a bike seat that opens up with just

00:49:50.920 --> 00:49:53.410
enough room for
wallet and the cell phone, so you can take

00:49:53.410 --> 00:49:56.650
those out of your
pocket and stick in into the bike seats. And

00:49:56.650 --> 00:50:00.720
there were
whole of little things like that. The brakes

00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:02.410
were not
handbrakes, you just paddled backwards to

00:50:02.410 --> 00:50:05.660
brake. There is an
automatic transmission which was Shimano technology

00:50:05.660 --> 00:50:09.079
which
adjusts the torque, which adjusts the gear,

00:50:09.079 --> 00:50:14.320
the gear ratio
based on torque on the chain and so on. So

00:50:14.320 --> 00:50:19.140
the point here
that I want to make is that the Coaster Bikes

00:50:19.140 --> 00:50:22.750
which they
came out with were entirely inspired by field

00:50:22.750 --> 00:50:25.320
research that
we did. They weren't any white papers or anything

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:29.490
that told
us, hey, there are tens of millions of Americans

00:50:29.490 --> 00:50:31.570
that are
intimated by the state of biking and that

00:50:31.570 --> 00:50:38.060
want something
simpler. I don't know how we would have to

00:50:38.060 --> 00:50:41.670
come to this
observation or to this conclusion without

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:46.339
going out in the
field. So now, let's talk about Synthesis.

00:50:46.339 --> 00:50:49.181
This is the last
point that Dave wanted me to make and I wanted

00:50:49.181 --> 00:50:52.550
to talk about
how we gather information before I talk about

00:50:52.550 --> 00:50:57.210
Synthesis. So,
let's imagine that we have done what I have

00:50:57.210 --> 00:51:01.280
described,
right. You have created a small team of T-shaped

00:51:01.280 --> 00:51:05.359
people. You
have a voice of the human, you have the voice

00:51:05.359 --> 00:51:09.599
of the user,
you have the voice of business or policy and

00:51:09.599 --> 00:51:12.050
you have a
voice of technology. You have gone out in

00:51:12.050 --> 00:51:14.210
the field, you
have read some white papers, you have talked

00:51:14.210 --> 00:51:20.770
to experts, you
have done some field research. And again to

00:51:20.770 --> 00:51:23.869
the question
that came up earlier about innovating on limited

00:51:23.869 --> 00:51:27.900
means, this
can be done very quickly and very inexpensively,

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:29.800
I am not
talking about commissioning six months of

00:51:29.800 --> 00:51:33.050
research. The
types of activities I am talking about here

00:51:33.050 --> 00:51:37.380
take weeks, not
months. What's next? The next thing to do

00:51:37.380 --> 00:51:41.320
is to use your
inspiration to visualize possibilities and

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:48.490
that's the idea
of Synthesis. So here is the premise. If we

00:51:48.490 --> 00:51:51.540
want to
innovate, if we want to come up with something

00:51:51.540 --> 00:51:59.440
new, we are
going to take in a lot of concrete data. So

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:07.290
if this is time
and this is relatively more concrete information

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:15.280
and this is
more abstract. We are going to take in a lot

00:52:15.280 --> 00:52:21.400
of data, some
of it is intended to inspire, some of it is

00:52:21.400 --> 00:52:26.040
intended to do
inform, whatever the case, it's about your

00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:30.040
tangible inputs.
What do we want to come out with? We want

00:52:30.040 --> 00:52:41.020
to come out with
concepts that win in the market. 

00:52:41.020 --> 00:52:44.390
People misapply
quantitative needs based user research, of

00:52:44.390 --> 00:52:46.971
the sort that I
described a little earlier. You need to be

00:52:46.971 --> 00:52:50.990
careful of this.
People draw a short circuit from here to here.

00:52:50.990 --> 00:52:57.810
I guarantee
you it won't work. If Louise is trying to

00:52:57.810 --> 00:53:02.339
come up with a new
software package for the medical instruments

00:53:02.339 --> 00:53:06.570
that his
company is developing, if he asks stakeholders

00:53:06.570 --> 00:53:09.400
what they
need and one person says, well I need a different

00:53:09.400 --> 00:53:11.060
kind of
home screen and someone else says, I need

00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:13.650
this sort of
button, if he takes that stuff literally,

00:53:13.650 --> 00:53:16.490
these inputs
literally and he just uses them to create

00:53:16.490 --> 00:53:19.320
an engineer
requirements spec and implements them, things

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:27.310
aren't going
to work. You must veer into a layer of abstraction

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:34.140
in order
to innovate successfully 

00:53:34.140 --> 00:53:37.960
and that's what Synthesis is.
Synthesis says, let's take all the inputs

00:53:37.960 --> 00:53:40.890
that we have been
gained in trying to understand what the opportunities

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:42.800
are
like. And you are doing your job right if

00:53:42.800 --> 00:53:46.339
you get divergent
inputs. If one person says this home screen

00:53:46.339 --> 00:53:49.060
is too
complicated and the other says, it's too simple,

00:53:49.060 --> 00:53:51.329
it needs
more features, you are doing your job, right,

00:53:51.329 --> 00:53:53.859
because you
are finding these irreconcilable differences

00:53:53.859 --> 00:53:57.580
and it's going
to be your job to reconcile them and to find

00:53:57.580 --> 00:54:05.180
a happy medium
between them. So here are the inputs. The

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:13.760
process of
Synthesis leads to insights, which in turn,

00:54:13.760 --> 00:54:21.310
leads to design
principles, which in turn, leads to concepts.

00:54:21.310 --> 00:54:29.750
What are some
examples of each of these? The insights that

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:32.900
David and I put
together and the rest of the team on this

00:54:32.900 --> 00:54:38.589
crafting project
were this, we created some personas, who are

00:54:38.589 --> 00:54:41.630
amalgams of
some of the most common and consisting user

00:54:41.630 --> 00:54:44.930
needs that we
saw in the field for five different types

00:54:44.930 --> 00:54:52.260
of crafters and we
identified two of those personas as targets

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:55.220
to fairly common
marketing method. We identified two of those

00:54:55.220 --> 00:55:00.770
personas as
ideal targets for the organization. The insights

00:55:00.770 --> 00:55:02.640
for the
bike project that I showed earlier had to

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:06.470
do with the
hesitation that so many Americans felt about

00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:12.750
biking. From
the insights, you come up with design principles

00:55:12.750 --> 00:55:14.880
and then
you know that the concepts that you come up

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:22.690
with have been
guided by a synthesized set of inputs. So

00:55:22.690 --> 00:55:25.150
the point of our
talk is about the "how of" innovation how

00:55:25.150 --> 00:55:31.290
do you synthesize.
So here we are there are seven of us and we

00:55:31.290 --> 00:55:37.300
are trying to
get all of our insights into some kind of

00:55:37.300 --> 00:55:43.020
actionable form.
So what's Synthesis, Synthesis says, let's

00:55:43.020 --> 00:55:47.339
take all this
input, let's find direction and make meaning

00:55:47.339 --> 00:55:50.590
from all this
disparate input that we have got. So I would

00:55:50.590 --> 00:55:52.722
tell you
exactly how we do at IDO and obviously, I

00:55:52.722 --> 00:55:56.460
am not saying that
you should graph this method into your organization,

00:55:56.460 --> 00:55:58.490
but
what you may want to do is take some of the

00:55:58.490 --> 00:56:02.950
general
principles and apply them as appropriate.

00:56:02.950 --> 00:56:07.950
Take 3-to-5-person
innovation team say everybody in this first

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:10.970
row, we all sit
together having done some field research.

00:56:10.970 --> 00:56:13.710
We have read some
whitepapers, we have gone out in the field

00:56:13.710 --> 00:56:17.630
and we have
talked to stakeholders, take posted notes,

00:56:17.630 --> 00:56:23.730
our goal, other
issues aside at IDO, is to keep them in business

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:28.190
with posted
notes. Each of us writes down key points and

00:56:28.190 --> 00:56:31.750
key inputs or
key insights rather from the research that

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:36.390
we have done. So
Dave had met somebody in the field, he writes

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:41.910
down 6, 8, 10
signature details of that individual to help

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:44.930
us understand
who that person is. The single best way to

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:47.680
be inspired is to
meet someone in the field, who is a potential

00:56:47.680 --> 00:56:51.099
user of your
product or service. A close second is to have

00:56:51.099 --> 00:56:54.350
your colleague
who did the same research, of the same research

00:56:54.350 --> 00:56:57.040
plan, tell
you about the person they met, show you a

00:56:57.040 --> 00:57:00.089
digital camera
image of who it is and put a bunch of notes

00:57:00.089 --> 00:57:03.500
up, in much of
same way that a novelist never gives you a

00:57:03.500 --> 00:57:07.890
complete extended
labored picture of a character in a novel.

00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:11.710
A novelist gives
you a few carefully chosen key signature details

00:57:11.710 --> 00:57:15.010
that add up
to a complete and holistic picture. You want

00:57:15.010 --> 00:57:16.900
to try to do
the same thing. You want to try to give the

00:57:16.900 --> 00:57:19.490
signature
details of the people that you met, so that

00:57:19.490 --> 00:57:21.790
we really
understand who they are, how they are behaving,

00:57:21.790 --> 00:57:24.540
what their
motivations are, questions like what are their

00:57:24.540 --> 00:57:28.440
attitudes
towards technology, how much media they consume,

00:57:28.440 --> 00:57:40.540
these sorts
of things. So step one is absorb the stories

00:57:40.540 --> 00:57:43.339
and tell
stories from the field. So in this case, the

00:57:43.339 --> 00:57:47.000
six of us say,
we would put posted notes on the wall, we

00:57:47.000 --> 00:57:50.400
would cover
potentially all these walls, sharing with

00:57:50.400 --> 00:57:55.849
each other who we
saw in the field. It is not a lengthy process.

00:57:55.849 --> 00:58:01.839
Next, the
best thing I can tell you and so this is,

00:58:01.839 --> 00:58:08.480
look for
repetition. Let's imagine that in aggregate,

00:58:08.480 --> 00:58:12.040
this group here
meets 16 people total. How could you possibly

00:58:12.040 --> 00:58:16.590
base a major
decision on 16 people? The answer is, you

00:58:16.590 --> 00:58:19.620
choose the right
people. You choose a set of attributes that

00:58:19.620 --> 00:58:24.170
are important to
you, orthogonal attributes of people. Typical

00:58:24.170 --> 00:58:28.950
attributes
might be age or life stage, socio-economic

00:58:28.950 --> 00:58:33.940
level, attitudes
towards technology and then you get into more

00:58:33.940 --> 00:58:37.230
specifics, are
they heavy media consumers, are they crafters,

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:41.810
do they have
high engagement or low engagement with money,

00:58:41.810 --> 00:58:44.540
whatever it
is. And then we will decide that David is

00:58:44.540 --> 00:58:51.420
going to go out in
the field that he is going to find a senior

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:55.119
citizen, who has
a lot of knowledge about technology, but very,

00:58:55.119 --> 00:58:58.099
very low
engagement with media, that's a rather interesting

00:58:58.099 --> 00:59:05.940
profile.
By searching a sufficiently broad set of people,

00:59:05.940 --> 00:59:09.730
even if it'
s a rather small number, you would be amazed

00:59:09.730 --> 00:59:13.099
how often
qualitative research performed with a thousand

00:59:13.099 --> 00:59:17.869
or more
people often bears out the results of qualitative

00:59:17.869 --> 00:59:20.910
research.
And I'll tell you that with say 8 or 10 or

00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:24.069
16 well-chosen
people, who are orthogonal to each other across

00:59:24.069 --> 00:59:26.069
the number
of attributes, they are distinct from each

00:59:26.069 --> 00:59:28.620
other around a
number of personal attitudes. I'll tell you

00:59:28.620 --> 00:59:32.650
what, if you
have met 12 people in the field and you are

00:59:32.650 --> 00:59:35.950
trying to
innovate for them all and you identify a particular

00:59:35.950 --> 00:59:38.890
theme or
opportunity or area of interest of say 8 or

00:59:38.890 --> 00:59:42.900
9 of those 12,
that is significant, that matters. Forget

00:59:42.900 --> 00:59:48.520
the fact that it's
not statistically significant. It's important.

00:59:48.520 --> 00:59:53.950
So that's all
to justify step 2, which is identifying patterns.

00:59:53.950 --> 00:59:56.140
Next,
extract the key insights. So now we are out

00:59:56.140 --> 00:59:59.000
here, now we are
thinking about insights and design principles.

00:59:59.000 --> 01:00:01.170
This is
really, really hard, because you feel like

01:00:01.170 --> 01:00:03.700
whatever you are
coming the way with is really tried. We did

01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:08.830
a project, we
worked for six weeks doing research for South

01:00:08.830 --> 01:00:13.849
Korean telecom
called SK Telecom on the use of how mobile

01:00:13.849 --> 01:00:16.349
phones could
serve the needs of tourists, pleasure travelers

01:00:16.349 --> 01:00:20.089
better. We
did research in Asia, Western Europe, US,

01:00:20.089 --> 01:00:23.369
tons of Synthesis.
One of the insights that we presented to them,

01:00:23.369 --> 01:00:26.819
we stood up
and said, there is an underserved appetite

01:00:26.819 --> 01:00:30.570
for authentic and
personalized travel advice. Of course there

01:00:30.570 --> 01:00:31.869
is, what a
ridiculous

01:00:31.869 --> 01:00:34.720
[inaudible] thing to say. But the fact is
there were lots of

01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:37.420
thinking and lots of interviews and lots of
information

01:00:37.420 --> 01:00:40.490
gathering behind that and the insight was
essentially a

01:00:40.490 --> 01:00:44.650
headline or the tip of an iceberg to a lot
of knowledge that

01:00:44.650 --> 01:00:47.609
had been gained by the team. Last point I
want to make

01:00:47.609 --> 01:00:50.800
speaks to decision that came about innovating
in big

01:00:50.800 --> 01:00:54.980
companies. Craft your message, it's tempting
to believe that

01:00:54.980 --> 01:00:57.941
having done field research and Synthesis and
brainstorming

01:00:57.941 --> 01:01:00.510
and identified an exciting direction, it's
tempting to

01:01:00.510 --> 01:01:03.119
believe that the IDO will sell itself and
that people will

01:01:03.119 --> 01:01:05.980
immediately see the benefits of the idea.
This particularly

01:01:05.980 --> 01:01:09.050
happens if you are personally excited about
your idea okay.

01:01:09.050 --> 01:01:12.270
Guess what, that doesn't happen and the message
needs to be

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:15.720
crafted in an appropriate way. For what I
am telling you

01:01:15.720 --> 01:01:18.310
here PowerPoint is a good message. PowerPoint
is always the

01:01:18.310 --> 01:01:20.530
right way to go. So at times you want to pay
a graphic

01:01:20.530 --> 01:01:23.790
artist to sketch up a storyboard to describe
the key touch

01:01:23.790 --> 01:01:27.410
points of a new service. Sometimes you want
to follow a

01:01:27.410 --> 01:01:30.160
quick video to simulate the actual experience
you are

01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:31.930
describing, sometimes you want to bring in
someone who is an

01:01:31.930 --> 01:01:39.140
expert in Flash, the software to assemble
a simulation. But

01:01:39.140 --> 01:01:41.560
just be aware that crafting the message particularly
in a

01:01:41.560 --> 01:01:43.530
large company where things have to go through
layer after

01:01:43.530 --> 01:01:46.329
layer of management and tollgate review after
tollgate

01:01:46.329 --> 01:01:50.180
review. Crafting a message is terribly important.
The last

01:01:50.180 --> 01:01:53.490
point I will make quickly is Synthesis needs
very naturally

01:01:53.490 --> 01:01:56.440
into brainstorming. I don't think brainstorming
is a very

01:01:56.440 --> 01:01:58.849
good idea in the earlier stages of the program,
because you

01:01:58.849 --> 01:02:01.440
don't know where the opportunities are yet.
If we are going

01:02:01.440 --> 01:02:04.710
to ask 6 to 8 people to get together to brainstorm
and a

01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:08.369
brainstorm is structured means of rapid idea
generation, we

01:02:08.369 --> 01:02:10.910
want to make sure that we have an appropriate
target. We

01:02:10.910 --> 01:02:12.670
want to make sure that we have got a framework
of

01:02:12.670 --> 01:02:15.530
opportunities and we have done our needs-based
research, so

01:02:15.530 --> 01:02:18.569
we have an idea of where to focus people's
attention. Hey we

01:02:18.569 --> 01:02:20.630
want it really to brainstorm on this sort
of next generation

01:02:20.630 --> 01:02:25.000
data service, hey we want everybody to focus
on this

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:33.270
approach to clinics in emerging economies,
wherever it is.

01:02:33.270 --> 01:02:37.710
One quick example, I mean I couldn't tell
you in 20 years in

01:02:37.710 --> 01:02:40.710
IDO, the coolest project I have ever worked
on, but I can

01:02:40.710 --> 01:02:45.400
tell you the biggest honor and it was this
for sure. A quick

01:02:45.400 --> 01:02:48.980
example of observation leading into Synthesis
was a program

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:55.800
for NASA on how to improve spacesuits. They
called and they

01:02:55.800 --> 01:03:00.540
said, can you work with us to help understand
how to improve

01:03:00.540 --> 01:03:03.069
space suits for the next generation of Lunar
and Martian

01:03:03.069 --> 01:03:07.710
exploration. And I kind of thought, well yeah,
I am willing

01:03:07.710 --> 01:03:15.730
to work on that. So I told NASA that the work
had to be done

01:03:15.730 --> 01:03:19.569
on our terms, that is, it had to be done using
IDO's

01:03:19.569 --> 01:03:23.430
approach to innovation and we needed to speak
to multiple

01:03:23.430 --> 01:03:26.540
stakeholders. And just to cut to the chase,
I walked into a

01:03:26.540 --> 01:03:29.619
room, it was about this size, and I couldn't
really believe

01:03:29.619 --> 01:03:32.570
until I actually showed up that we are able
to coordinate

01:03:32.570 --> 01:03:36.500
all this. With the help of leadership at NASA,
we got

01:03:36.500 --> 01:03:40.500
astronauts from three generations of space
travel. We got

01:03:40.500 --> 01:03:44.180
men who walked on the moon, men who were in
Skylab in the

01:03:44.180 --> 01:03:48.349
70s and men and women who had been to the
international

01:03:48.349 --> 01:03:52.750
space station. We even got this dapper guy
in his 70s, who

01:03:52.750 --> 01:03:57.000
designed the spacesuits for the Apollo astronauts.
He was

01:03:57.000 --> 01:04:01.109
the design engineer and his name, true story,
is Joe Kosmo,

01:04:01.109 --> 01:04:09.740
so I just love. So this is an example of the
innovation

01:04:09.740 --> 01:04:15.920
process in action. Spacesuits are disaster.
When I got into

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:18.210
one, the only thing you can think of when
you get into a

01:04:18.210 --> 01:04:20.680
spacesuit is how soon you can get out of the
thing. Picture

01:04:20.680 --> 01:04:23.849
a ski boot, not a snowboarding boot, but for
those of you

01:04:23.849 --> 01:04:26.640
who have had parallel ski, the ski boot that
doesn't fit you

01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:29.559
and it like extend that over your whole body.
It's like the

01:04:29.559 --> 01:04:33.010
worst feeling you can possibly imagine. Or
if you wear

01:04:33.010 --> 01:04:37.400
contact lenses, imagine a hard contact lens
with a piece of

01:04:37.400 --> 01:04:39.430
dust under it, where all you can think about
is getting it

01:04:39.430 --> 01:04:45.440
out of your eye. That's how a spacesuit feels.
And just to

01:04:45.440 --> 01:04:47.470
cut to the chase, it was a wonderful program,
one of the

01:04:47.470 --> 01:04:51.910
things we concluded was that in ten-plus year
timeframe,

01:04:51.910 --> 01:04:56.420
material science was still long ways away
from solving the

01:04:56.420 --> 01:04:59.359
challenge of creating something was more comfortable,
had

01:04:59.359 --> 01:05:02.610
improved situational awareness and was safer
in

01:05:02.610 --> 01:05:04.970
consideration of all the things a spacesuit
has to do. It

01:05:04.970 --> 01:05:07.480
has to be a pressure vessel, it has to keep
astronauts cool,

01:05:07.480 --> 01:05:11.359
when they are in the sun, warm when they in
the shade. It

01:05:11.359 --> 01:05:15.579
needs micrometeorite protection. It needs
to provide air and

01:05:15.579 --> 01:05:17.880
other nutrients. It has to monitor a lot of
physiological

01:05:17.880 --> 01:05:23.410
processes. And through the process of talking
to astronauts

01:05:23.410 --> 01:05:26.690
and through the process of brainstorming with
them and being

01:05:26.690 --> 01:05:30.300
art-directed by them, the particular insight
in this case

01:05:30.300 --> 01:05:33.960
was, we can make some improvements to spacesuits,
but really

01:05:33.960 --> 01:05:37.820
the right answer is to create something that
provides a lot

01:05:37.820 --> 01:05:40.230
of the most important attributes of a spacesuit
like

01:05:40.230 --> 01:05:44.099
situational awareness, but allows astronauts
to work in a

01:05:44.099 --> 01:05:47.850
shirt-sleeve environment. So what we came
up with was --

01:05:47.850 --> 01:05:54.380
these are some of the early sketches that
we made of a very

01:05:54.380 --> 01:05:59.630
small pressurized rover that would allow astronauts
to work

01:05:59.630 --> 01:06:02.280
inside of it. It would allow them to slip
in and out of the

01:06:02.280 --> 01:06:05.980
spacesuits very, very quickly in ten minutes
rather than an

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:12.109
hour that it takes today. And if you saw some
of those

01:06:12.109 --> 01:06:15.320
sketches a second ago, you can see if you
look at the actual

01:06:15.320 --> 01:06:17.839
prototype. On the left, it's being presented
to President

01:06:17.839 --> 01:06:20.940
Obama's inauguration and on the right, it's
being tested in

01:06:20.940 --> 01:06:24.260
Arizona. You can see that the actual prototype
is pretty

01:06:24.260 --> 01:06:27.510
close to some of the earliest sketches that
we have made as

01:06:27.510 --> 01:06:32.790
a result of brainstorming and Synthesis with
this team of

01:06:32.790 --> 01:06:38.740
astronauts. So I am going to stop there. I
would like to

01:06:38.740 --> 01:06:42.589
know what questions you have or comments you
have about the

01:06:42.589 --> 01:07:05.171
process of Synthesis. Yeah.

01:07:05.171 --> 01:07:06.171
[Audience]

01:07:06.171 --> 01:07:14.460
So in general, if you are outsourcing professional
services,

01:07:14.460 --> 01:07:17.800
the more upstream you are, the more upstream
your

01:07:17.800 --> 01:07:22.830
outsourcing activity is, the more what we
call radical

01:07:22.830 --> 01:07:27.839
collaboration is essential. So I agree with
that statement,

01:07:27.839 --> 01:07:34.160
if the professional services are managed.
If the founders of

01:07:34.160 --> 01:07:38.809
a startup commission a market research fund
to identify the

01:07:38.809 --> 01:07:41.119
market come up with some concepts and then
come back with "

01:07:41.119 --> 01:07:44.490
the answer." And they disappear for six weeks
and come back

01:07:44.490 --> 01:07:47.230
with something. I guarantee without knowing
any more

01:07:47.230 --> 01:07:52.780
details, I guarantee it will be a failure.
The only way to

01:07:52.780 --> 01:07:56.140
outsource successfully on early upstream program
is what we

01:07:56.140 --> 01:07:59.980
call Radical Collaboration. And what distinguishes
radical

01:07:59.980 --> 01:08:02.630
collaboration from other types of collaboration
is this, in

01:08:02.630 --> 01:08:06.599
radical collaboration, innovation programs
shared generative

01:08:06.599 --> 01:08:14.349
activities occurred. What that means is that
in the

01:08:14.349 --> 01:08:22.579
hypothetical you just gave, the startup identifies
particular points of engagement with the firm

01:08:22.579 --> 01:08:25.460
that they have
outsourced some innovation activities too.

01:08:25.460 --> 01:08:27.880
They spent a day
with them, two days, three days, at all the

01:08:27.880 --> 01:08:32.540
key points. And
if you go on that shared process of discovery,

01:08:32.540 --> 01:08:36.589
you are going
to be okay. It's when external entities go

01:08:36.589 --> 01:08:40.400
open loop during
early stages that you get in the trouble.

01:08:40.400 --> 01:08:44.339
This rule doesn't
apply once you get more into implementation.

01:08:44.339 --> 01:08:47.920
You can
successfully create engineering requirement

01:08:47.920 --> 01:08:52.420
steps and
detailed specifications, ship those overseas

01:08:52.420 --> 01:08:55.010
and gotten very
good code back from peoplem whom we have never

01:08:55.010 --> 01:08:58.160
met in,
Bangalore. That's possible once

01:08:58.160 --> 01:09:02.009
[inaudible] unambiguous and defined. Early
in the process,

01:09:02.009 --> 01:09:05.630
it isn't. So back to specifics, if you think
about these

01:09:05.630 --> 01:09:11.750
phases I just described, we always include
our clients going

01:09:11.750 --> 01:09:15.339
into the field with us to do the field research.
We include

01:09:15.339 --> 01:09:17.529
them and they have what we call a seat at
the Synthesis

01:09:17.529 --> 01:09:20.920
table. They are sitting with us in brainstorms.
That's

01:09:20.920 --> 01:09:23.819
essential. Other questions?

01:09:23.819 --> 01:09:28.500
And I think, well let me just say, of those
